## Why This Is Asked

This question probes an advanced testing scenario where data consistency is eventual and involves a read-model updated by a streaming pipeline, requiring tests around read-your-writes, consistency window, multi-tenant isolation, and resilience to streaming outages.

## Key Concepts

- Eventual consistency between read-model and stream
- Read-your-writes and drift windows
- Per-tenant isolation under concurrency
- Streaming fault injection and recovery
- Contract testing across gateway and downstream services

## Code Example

```javascript
// Example test sketch: verify eventual consistency
async function testReadYourWrites(tenantId, value){
  await writeMetric(tenantId, value);
  await untilTrue(async ()=> {
     const v = await readMetrics(tenantId);
     return v.includes(value);
  }, 30000);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you measure the real-world consistency window, and what metrics would you collect?
- How would you design roll-out and rollback strategies if drift exceeds SLA?

## Why This Is Asked
Payments APIs rely on idempotency to avoid duplicates; beginners must reason about retries, concurrency, and race conditions.

## Key Concepts
- Idempotency keys
- Retry semantics and concurrency
- Conflict handling with identical keys
- Downstream gateway behavior and fallbacks

## Code Example
```javascript
// Example test outline (pseudo)
test('idempotent payment', async () => {
  const key = 'abc-123';
  const payload = {amount: 100, currency: 'USD', customerId: 'C1'};
  const r1 = await post('/payments', payload, {'Idempotency-Key': key});
  const r2 = await post('/payments', payload, {'Idempotency-Key': key});
  assert(r1.paymentId === r2.paymentId);
});
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt tests for bulk or async payments?
- How would you simulate high contention on the idempotency-key path?

## Why This Is Asked
Testing idempotent payment flows under bank failures is critical for reliability and user trust.

## Key Concepts
- Idempotency and Idempotency-Key semantics
- End-to-end testing with external banks
- Race conditions and concurrency
- Failure injection and retry/backoff strategies
- Data reconciliation and compensation patterns

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal skeleton for an idempotent charge test
async function testIdempotentCharge(client, amount, key) {
  const r1 = await client.post('/payments/charge', { amount }, { headers: { 'Idempotency-Key': key } })
  const r2 = await client.post('/payments/charge', { amount }, { headers: { 'Idempotency-Key': key } })
  if (r1.chargeId !== r2.chargeId) throw new Error('Idempotency violated')
  if (r2.status !== 'succeeded') throw new Error('Unexpected status')
}
```

